Here's a quick self-contained test you can run with Node (`node zip-test.js`):

```javascript
const zipRegex = /^\d{5}(?:-\d{4})?$/;

const cases = [
  // [input, expected]
  ["12345", true],
  ["90210", true],
  ["12345-6789", true],
  ["1234", false],
  ["123456", false],
  ["12345-", false],
  ["12345-678", false],
  ["12345-67890", false],
  ["123456789", false],
  ["abcde", false],
  ["12345 ", false],
  ["", false],
];

let failed = 0;

for (const [input, expected] of cases) {
  const actual = zipRegex.test(input);
  const ok = actual === expected;
  if (!ok) failed++;
  console.log(`${ok ? "PASS" : "FAIL"}  ${JSON.stringify(input)} -> ${actual}`);
}

console.log(failed === 0 ? "\nAll tests passed" : `\n${failed} test(s) failed`);
process.exitCode = failed === 0 ? 0 : 1;
```

**Notes:**
- In JavaScript, `\d` only matches ASCII digits `0-9`, so you don't need `[0-9]` the way you might in Python or .NET.
- Unlike Python, JS `$` (without the `m` flag) matches only at the very end of the string, so `"12345\n"` correctly fails. You can add `["12345\n", false]` to the cases to confirm.
- Avoid the `g` flag on a regex you reuse with `.test()`. It keeps `lastIndex` state between calls and can produce alternating true/false results.

If you use a test framework like Jest or Vitest, the same cases work with `test.each`:

```javascript
test.each(cases)("zip %j -> %s", (input, expected) => {
  expect(zipRegex.test(input)).toBe(expected);
});
```

